{"headline": "France braces for fresh student demonstrations as Macron calls for 'respect'", "essay": "Three weeks in, the numbers are moving the wrong way. School closures fell to 130 of France's 3,700 lycees. The education ministry's own read: the violence is intensifying. Eight teachers were injured in a single morning across Paris, Bordeaux and Lyon. Police have opened 24 internal brutality investigations. More than 6,800 people, mostly minors, have been taken into custody. One protester lost a hand to a stun grenade, another an eye.

The grievances are unglamorous: absent teachers, packed classes, crumbling rooms. The government offered 3,000 substitute teachers for the 114 worst schools. The national students' union called it crumbs and asked for 10 billion euros instead. Macron, at the Paris Motor Show, asked for respect and time.

My view: when a state answers a maintenance backlog with policing, the backlog stops being the story."}

{"headline": "North Korea fires multiple ballistic missiles, adding to streak of weapons tests", "essay": "North Korea fired from Pyongyang again on Monday, and this time the interesting part is the shape of the flight. South Korea's military said missiles of various types went toward the eastern waters, one traveling more than 900 kilometers. Japan counted at least two, launched 17 minutes apart, both landing outside its exclusive economic zone. The second flew more than 910 kilometers on what Japan's deputy defense minister called an irregular path, language that points to a hypersonic weapon built to dodge interceptors.

Timing matters. South Korea flight-tested its own hypersonic weapon three days earlier. Seoul's military read Monday's launches as a technology push and a reply.

My view: the arms race is now about evasion, not range, and each test teaches the other side how to defend."}

